# TIME(1)

## NAME

time - time a command

## SYNOPSIS

| **time** command [ argument ... ]

## DESCRIPTION

The given command is executed; after it is complete, *time* prints the elapsed time during the
command, the time spent in the system, and the time spent in execution of the command. Times
are reported in hours, minutes, seconds and tenths of a second, with leading zeros suppressed.

**Note:** v7's page said `Times are reported in seconds`, which was never quite what the
program printed.

The times are printed on the diagnostic output stream.

The exit status is the command's own. **Note:** v7's page did not say so.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### A tick is a 250th of a second, not a 60th

Times(2) counts in 1/HZ seconds and **HZ is 250 here,** where the PDP-11's line clock ran at
60\. Both places v7's source built that number in - the conversion of elapsed seconds to ticks,
and the leading entry of the radix table that decodes ticks back into tenths - are derived from
**CLOCKS_PER_SEC** now. The printed layout is unchanged.

### The separators are no longer NUL bytes

v7's *printt* held \\0 in its separator tables to mean `nothing between these two digits`, and
printed it anyway. On a terminal that is invisible, which is the only place v7 looked; in a
file it is a NUL, which is what **time cmd 2>log** produces. Fixed: the separator is emitted
only when there is one.

### The paragraph about MOS and core is gone

It said that execution time depends on which kind of memory the program lands in, and that user
time in MOS is often half what it is in core. That is PDP-11/45 hardware, and this machine has
none of it.

## BUGS

Elapsed time is accurate to the second, being the difference of two time(2) calls, while the
CPU times are measured to the 250th. Thus the sum of the CPU times can be up to a second larger
than the elapsed time.

## SEE ALSO

times(2), time(2)
